  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nLast night, light rain moved through the coastal basin, tapering off \nearly this morning. Overnight accumulations generally totaled less \nthan 0.15\" with up to 0.5\" in foothill and mountain locations. \n\nExpect mostly cloudy skies across the region for the rest of the \nday, with a few light, widely scattered showers and a few peeks of \nsunshine. Showers taper off everywhere overnight except for \ncoastal areas, which could continue to see light showers through \nSaturday morning. \n\nSaturday will remain overcast across the region, though uncertainty \nremains regarding the onset of widespread precipitation. \n\nCurrent high-resolution guidance generally delays widespread \nrainfall until late Saturday night, potentially near or after \nmidnight. However, the 00z Sunday GFS soundings off the coast depict \nan already saturated mid and upper level atmosphere with a classic \nmid-level warm frontal inversion. This suggests showers could \ndevelop as early as late Saturday afternoon, with more widespread \nrainfall spreading across the coastal basin by Halloween evening. \nMeanwhile, confidence still remain high that precipitation across \nthe Coachella Valley and lower deserts will hold off until the \novernight hours of Saturday into Sunday. \n\nPrecipitation Saturday night will start off light and steady, with \nembedded pockets of moderate to locally heavy rain during the early \nhours of Sunday. Later Sunday morning, widespread rainfall is \nexpected to transition to scattered showers across the coastal \nbasin, with precipitation ending in the deserts. Showers become \nincreasingly isolated through Sunday afternoon, except along the San \nDiego County foothills and mountains, where more persistent activity \nwill continue. Most precipitation should come to an end by Sunday \nnight with mostly sunny skies returning region-wide by Monday.\n\nThe first few hours of precipitation will feature mainly consistent \nlight rain region-wide with perhaps a few pockets of moderate, \nlocally heavy rain in the very early hours of Sunday. Sunday \nmorning, widespread rain scatters out to showers for the coastal \nbasin (no more desert rain), becoming widely scattered by the \nafternoon except along the San Diego county foothills and mountains. \nMost rainfall will be wrapped up by Sunday night with mostly sunny \nskies expected region-wide by Monday. \n\nAs the storm is tracking to intersect over Northern Baja California, \nthe higher precipitation totals will be focused over San Diego \nCounty (0.4-0.7\"), notably near the US/Mexico Border. Elsewhere, \ntotals are more likely to range between 0.25-0.50\" (including \ndeserts) with locally higher totals in the mountains.\n\nAs the storm clear the region, low temperatures will drop \nMonday/Tuesday morning to the upper-40s inland and down into the low \n30s and 20s in elevated mountain valleys.\n\nNext week, a series of weak, fast-moving ridges and troughs will \nkeep temperatures fluctuation slightly - highs will range from \nthe low to mid-70s daily in the coastal basin and mid-70s to \nlow-80s in the Coachella Valley (and other low desert valleys).",
